 \nThe safest discard should consider both defensive value (avoid feeding opponents) and strategic irrelevance.  \n- B5 appears in an opponent\u2019s meld ([B3, B4, B5]), making it potentially dangerous.  \n- B8 is part of your own meld and has been played before; however, two copies are already visible in your hand/meld, so third copy may still pose risk.  \n- W6: Given that the previous player completed [W6, W7, W8] and other W6/W5 are heavily used, it's highly likely safe. Also, you only have one W6 and cannot form a pair/pung with it.  \n- B9, F3, J1: These are unique tiles with only one remaining each. While not obviously dangerous, discarding them carries slight risk due to scarcity \u2014 someone may be waiting on them.  \n\nAmong options, **W6** stands out as the safest because:\n- Multiple W6s have already been exposed (in your meld and others\u2019 actions).\n- The suit is well-represented on the table, reducing its value.\n- You cannot pair it (only one in hand), so it serves no internal purpose.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
